and we have some sports news out of  afghanistan their female youth national  football team and their families a group  of 80 in total have been granted asylum  by portugal after trying to leave  afghanistan for weeks unsuccessfully the  group has now arrived in lisbon the  taliban have banned girls from playing  any sport and fearing what their lives  might become the team had been moving  between safe houses before being granted  asylum all of the girls aged between 14  and 16 want to become pro football  players in the future  as well as athletes afghan musicians are  also facing oppression under the new  taliban regime afghanistan's only  institute for music has closed down and  its members fear for their safety and  their future their director who is in  exile in australia after leaving the  country in july for medical treatment is  hoping to help  it was a building full of music hope and  optimism 250 students were enrolled at  the national institute of music in kabul  a third of them female a safe place to  practice laugh and discover the world  but the school was constantly under  threat  its director survived a suicide bomb  attack from exile in australia his  defiance is undimmed even as the taliban  have banned music in public  places still i believe that no one can  take the music from the life of a human  being  you can't kill music music is not going  to die  i am an ambassador from the land of hope  these girls sing i stand up against  oppression  [Music]  self-confident and passionate wearing  their national costume a young orchestra  with a female conductor at the podium  one of two that afghanistan's only music  school produced  a sound the taliban does not tolerate  that lack of  knowledge of this beautiful language is  probably based on the ignorance of the  taliban and also  on the narrow  narrow interpretation of the teaching of  holy islam there's not a single islamic  country around the world where music  could have been banned or music is  banned currently  the centerpiece of this kabul music  school is zora the first afghan women's  orchestra it played 47 concerts  worldwide in the last 10 years including  in berlin  the berlin philharmonic orchestra's horn  player welcomed the ensemble's members  on their visit to the capitol  we love playing music  not only because we like to make  beautiful sounds but because we can  express our souls with it and when you  play music you become one with the music  and if you're not allowed to do that  it's such a terrible thing  i just can't even imagine it  but the orchestra has stopped playing  even before the taliban seized power  conductor zarifa adiba could not take  her instrument home it was too dangerous  i don't have viola at home and  i never i never ever practiced viola at  home because  i'm afraid that anyone should know that  i'm playing viola  conductors arifa and her colleague nagin  are now safe in the u.s  all of the other orchestra members are  in hiding in kabul  from on that subject we can speak now to  the hornets we just saw in that report  sarah willis she's a horn player with a  berlin philharmonic and she's also hosts  sarah's music program here on dw sarah  what's gone through your mind with the  news from afghanistan this past month  especially when it comes to music  goodness well just hearing the report  just now and hearing the words of of  sarifa and dr samas i i just have to  think of this wonderful day i spent with  them and how impressed i was with the  bravery of these young girls because  okay they were very young some of them  and maybe the danger wasn't quite  um you know registered yet but they all  knew that what they were doing  could have consequences and they did it  anyway and i just admired them so much  and it's really breaking my heart to  hear these reports now  so as you said you met members of the  women's orchestra zora in berlin when  they were on tour here four years ago  tell us more about that encounter  well i showed them the philharmonie um  they were so impressed with the with the  concert hall and i was i was impressed  how much they knew about classical music  actually which is a a great sign that  they were getting very good training um  not just in the afghan music but also in  general general music training and uh we  had a very fun morning together and then  i accompanied them to their rehearsal in  their concert at the um at the memorial  church the goddess here in in berlin and  there just been that terror um attack on  the chair on the on the christmas market  there and they all went outside and laid  flowers and it was very moving to see to  see them doing something for us and uh  it was just a wonderful happy  music-filled day and they're young girls  you know they're chattering laughing  getting ready for the concerts and  they're so colorful when you see them in  their beautiful clothes that they were  wearing for the concerts and the joy of  music making it's just music just  connects and you could sense this joy  all the way through the concert  you mentioned that they are playing uh  classical western music as well as  afghan  music but classic classical music is  being played all around the world but  what could happen to the country's  musical heritage of afghanistan's  musical heritage when uh with the  government's so anti-music  this is the big question and of course  we don't know what's going to happen in  the future the reports of the last few  weeks are really sad and really worrying  um if it carries on like this then the  musical heritage of afghanistan will  really suffer because it's a whole  generation of music making but also  music teaching that will literally be be  silenced  and  if we are still allowed to hope we are  hoping it may not come to this but  it's not looking good as far as i can  see it at the moment  um and it's it's not only that this  music  needs to be learned that the instruments  that these wonderful traditional afghan  instruments the rhubarb for example and  the sitar and the tabla  they need to be taught and most of this  music isn't written down so it's taught  from you know from generation to  generation and if it's silence now how  are these girls going to pass it down  not even the girls the boys that were  learning it too how are they going to  pass it down to the next generation and  and a world without music a silent world  nobody wants that  and it's not only about music itself  isn't it i mean musical education means  so much more doesn't it  it certainly does it's this this feeling  of creating something together to be to  be expressing your soul um and music  learning music is very good for the  brain we we know that it's very good for  young children for their brain  development and also what was important  in afghanistan at the a a nim  was the um the fact that boys and girls  studied together and they learned about  the interaction together because they've  been separated and they were never  allowed to inter-interact like that so  um  you can see in some of the videos of the  school um they're sitting next to each  other playing together so that's an  important social aspect as well  sarah willis horn player with the berlin  philharmonic orchestra thank you very  much sarah  thank you
 

